A||||8||Wannabe||'88 Front Suspension Alignment||||1||
Z||000000||Wannabe||05-15-2001||11:09 PM||||Just curious....there doesn't appear to be any - no place for shims or eccentric bushings - adjustment for the upper control arm mounting on the front of the '88s. How would you adjust camber and caster?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||05-16-2001||01:42 AM||||The shaft is where the adjustment is made.  The two bolts are loosened and the entire conrol arm moved to where it nees to be......the frame rail that these bolts attach to are slotted.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever2||05-16-2001||02:15 AM||||Just had my 88 coupe aligned at Firestone.  Took a very long time.  The guy said these are the hardest cars to align.  Steering & suspension I've never learned, so I took his word for it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000003||Coop88||05-16-2001||04:39 AM||||I'll be getting my formula aligned after I'm done installing my poly bushings. Had to replace those shafts too. I've tried to have it aligned three times, and I've gotten three different excuses. Two of them came from the dealership. Hopefully the next shop I go to will know what they're doing. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroLT1||05-16-2001||07:44 AM||||Just took my Formula in to a GoodYear's for alignment and balance. It was Saturday, and there was only one guy on staff. He did the balance and poked around with the alignment for about an hour. He said it was going to take him another 4 hours to align and that I'd be better off to have it done with the "Hot Rod" specialist that was going to be in on Monday. And he sent me on my way.<BR>BTW, the idiot put the weights on the outside of the wheel.<P>------------------<BR>Winston Ojeda<BR><B>88 Formula LT1</B><BR>Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA<BR><A HREF="http://www.FieroLT1.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.FieroLT1.com</A><BR><I>Home of the Fiero LT1</I><BR>  <A HREF="mailto:wojeda@FieroLT1.com">wojeda@FieroLT1.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||05-16-2001||09:24 AM||||Sounds pretty straight forward if the mounting holes are slotted. I wonder what the confusion is about?||||reg||1
Z||000006||btoth||05-16-2001||11:00 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>BTW, the idiot put the weights on the outside of the wheel.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine are like that... looks dumb.  Next time I'm requesting that they put them on the back.||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||05-16-2001||11:26 AM||||I just had my '88 aligned last week. When I told the guy over the phone that is was a fiero, he said $80 and 2 hours. When I took the car to him, he looked arround, and said "I didn't think it was like this." I told him about the change in '88, $45 and 45 minutes later, I had a 4 wheel alignment finished. also found out that my rear tires were about 5 degrees off on the camber (thus the reason the rear tires were completely chewed) if you're in the northern VA area, this guy is close to Dale City (just off I95) - "Earl's Auto-Aling & Spin Balance" (703)221-1953. He does it all by hand (no lazers) but he's good at it. The problem w/ pre '88s that most shops fight you on is that they may have to file the slots into the control arm, thus taking lots of time and labor.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||05-16-2001||02:01 PM||||'88s are a lot easier to align than the early Fieros, buy most shops treat them the same.  <P>Way back in 1990 I brought in my '88 GT to Goodyear for an alignment.  They wanted to charge me $120 because they had to rotate the upper ball joints.  I left immediately! <P>Other shops don't like them because they do take a little longer to get the alignment just right.  Since you have found a place that will do them and do them right keep going there!  It is hard to find a competent shop.||||reg||1
